# Tutorial Dialogues - Tutorial dialogue sequences

# ===== INTRODUCTION SEQUENCE =====
label tutorial_start:
    scene expression workers_bg
    show expression Solid("#00000080")  # Semi-transparent black overlay
    
    "The scribe slides the final parchment across the worn wooden table. The ink glistens wet from the quill."
    
    "Scribe" "And with that, the property is officially yours. I'm... sorry for your loss."
    
    "Thank you."
    
    "The old man gathers his scrolls, clearly uncomfortable with the weight of history in this room."
    
    "Scribe" "If you need anything else, you know where to find me at the guild hall. Good fortune with your endeavors."
    
    "His robes rustle as the heavy wooden door closes with a dull thud."
    
    "Alone now, I stare at the deed in my hands. A single building - all that remains of my family's empire."
    
    "The governor made sure to destroy everything else. Seized our holdings, called in our debts, turned our allies against us..."
    
    "My reflection in the window glass shows my parent's eyes, filled with the same determination."
    
    "But they made a mistake leaving me this. From here, I'll rebuild what was lost."
    
    "I take the quill and sign my name at the bottom of the deed with deliberate strokes."
    
    return

# ===== OBJECTIVE DIALOGUES =====
label objective_1_dialogue:
    "Mine eyes survey the three souls who have sworn themselves to my cause. 'Tis but a modest beginning, yet from such humble seeds do mighty oaks grow."
    "Verily, three loyal servants now stand beneath my banner. Though their number be small, every empire that has ever risen began with but a single step upon the path to greatness."
    "Each doth bring their own gifts and talents to this endeavor, their own reasons for seeking shelter beneath my protection. Some came for the promise of coin, others drawn by opportunity's sweet song, and a few... well, they had precious little choice in the matter."
    "Now must I decree what manner of enterprise this hallowed place shall become. The governor built his dominion through commerce and influence - by such means shall I forge my own path to power."
    "Thy Journal hath been inscribed with thy next duty."
    return

# ===== BUILDING MECHANICS EXPLANATION =====
label building_mechanics_explanation:
    scene expression tavern_bg
    
    "Ere I expand my dominion further, I must comprehend the arts of stronghold management."
    
    "Every edifice possesses two cardinal virtues that determine its worthiness: Stature and Mastery."
    
    "I examine the ancient texts and ledgers I have gathered concerning the prosperous enterprises of this realm."
    
    "A building's Stature doth influence two matters of great import: the reverence it commands and the souls it may shelter."
    
    "I inscribe notes with careful deliberation as the implications become clear."
    
    "Edifices of greater stature generate more reverence amongst the populace. Such reverence opens doors that remain barred to lesser establishments, creates opportunities where none existed, and makes the people more willing to serve thy cause."
    
    "The governor's strongholds all stand tall with great stature - 'tis part of why they command such unwavering respect."
    
    "Stature also determines how many souls a building may house within its walls. A humble establishment of the first rank might shelter but a few servants, whilst a grand edifice of the fifth rank could house dozens of loyal subjects."
    
    "More servants means greater income, more services rendered, more influence wielded."
    
    "Then there are the Building's Masteries. These differ from the skills of individual workers - they represent the edifice's ability to weather challenges and overcome trials."
    
    "I recall the tales I have heard of enterprises facing unexpected tribulations."
    
    "When events occur that test a stronghold - inspections by the crown, attacks from rivals, natural calamities, political pressure - the building's masteries determine how well it shall endure."
    
    "An edifice with great mastery in 'Security' can better withstand criminal threats. High mastery in 'Diplomacy' aids in navigating political storms. Great mastery in 'Management' improves efficiency during times of crisis."
    
    "The governor's strongholds rarely fail such tests because he hath invested heavily in building masteries. 'Tis why his empire stands so stable and enduring."
    
    "I close the ledger, my strategy crystallizing like morning frost."
    
    "I must invest in both virtues. Greater stature for capacity and reverence, higher masteries for resilience and effectiveness."
    
    "'Tis thus that I shall build something worthy to challenge his empire."
    
    return

